    {
      "section": "legal.policy-header",
      "variant": "default",
      "content": {
        "title": "How Lattice approaches AI responsibly"
      },
      "motion": {
        "pattern": "static",
        "reducedMotionFallback": "none-needed"
      }
    },
    {
      "section": "legal.policy-body",
      "variant": "default",
      "content": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Why we write principles down at all"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "*Placeholder text for a fictional company. This page is not a real policy and has no legal effect.* It sketches how a software company might explain the rules it follows when it builds assistants and other AI features into its products. In this example, Lattice adds AI features to tools that people use to model and share data. Those features can draft code, suggest tests, explain unfamiliar parts of a project and answer questions about data. Because the answers can influence real decisions, the company wants anyone using them to know what to expect and what it will not do. These principles apply to every AI feature in the fictional product, whether it is generally available, in preview or still an experiment shared with a small group of design partners. They are reviewed every six months by a group that includes engineering, product, legal and security, and changes are announced in the release notes so that customers are never surprised."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Grounded in your data"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "An assistant is only useful when it understands the project it is working in. Lattice features are designed to read the models, descriptions, tests and metric definitions that a team has already written, rather than guessing from table names or general knowledge."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "When an answer depends on a definition, the assistant shows which model or metric it used, so a person can check the source. If the project does not contain enough information to answer, the assistant says so instead of inventing an answer. You can read more about how context is shared in the [context service overview](/product/assistant)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Customers decide what an assistant can see. Access follows the permissions of the person using it, and administrators can turn features off for a whole account or for individual projects. Every request made by an automated agent is logged with the question, the objects it read and the identity it acted for, and those logs are available to account administrators through the [audit settings](/security)."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "People stay in charge"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "AI features in this fictional product suggest; people decide. Generated code, tests and documentation arrive as drafts that go through the same review as any other change. Nothing an assistant writes reaches a production project without a person approving it, and automated agents are given read-only access by default. Teams can tighten these rules further, but the product will not loosen them."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Privacy"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Customer data used to answer a question stays within the customer’s account and is not used to train shared models. Prompts and responses are kept only as long as needed to provide the feature and to keep an audit trail, and customers can choose shorter retention periods."
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Where a feature relies on a third-party model provider, the provider is bound by contract to the same rules: no training on customer data, no retention beyond what the feature requires and processing only in the regions the customer has chosen. A current list of providers would be published here in a real policy, together with the regions in which each one processes data and the date each agreement was last reviewed."
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Measured quality"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Before an AI feature is released more widely, the fictional team measures how often it gives correct, useful answers on a set of realistic test projects, and how often it declines to answer when it should. Results are compared with the previous version, and a feature that gets worse on either measure does not ship until the cause is understood."
          },
          {
            "kind": "ul",
            "items": [
              {
                "text": "**Evaluation before release.** Every AI feature is tested against a library of example projects that reflect the kinds of work customers do, from small teams with a few dozen models to large companies with thousands. The library includes deliberately incomplete projects, so the team can check that the assistant admits when it does not know something instead of producing a confident guess.",
                "items": [
                  "Accuracy is measured against answers written and reviewed by experienced practitioners.",
                  "Refusals are measured on questions the project cannot answer."
                ]
              },
              {
                "text": "**Monitoring after release.** Once a feature is live, the team watches aggregate signals such as how often suggestions are accepted, edited or rejected, and how often people report a wrong answer. Individual prompts are not read by staff unless a customer shares them in a support request or explicitly opts in to a research programme. When a pattern of problems appears, the feature can be rolled back for everyone within minutes while the team investigates, and affected customers are told what happened and what changed.",
                "items": [
                  "Customers can report a wrong answer from inside the product with one click."
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "img",
            "image": {
              "assetRole": "figure.article-image",
              "alt": "Four principle cards: human review, clear sources, private by default, measured quality",
              "ref": "/assets/pages/legal/principles.svg"
            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "h3",
            "text": "Open about limits"
          },
          {
            "kind": "p",
            "text": "Every AI feature in this placeholder product carries a short note that explains what it is good at, what it is not designed for and where its answers come from. When those limits change, the note changes too, and the release notes say why."
          }
        ]
      },
